    {
      "text": "Sum rule for the Compton amplitude and implications for the proton-neutron mass difference — The Cottingham formula expresses the lead- ing contribution of the electromagnetic interaction to the\n\n**Sum rule for the Compton amplitude and implications for the proton-neutron mass difference**\n\n*J. Gasser1, H. Leutwyler1, A. Rusetsky2*\n\n26 pp, 34 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** The Cottingham formula expresses the lead- ing contribution of the electromagnetic interaction to the proton-neutron mass diﬀerence as an integral over the forward Compton amplitude. Since quarks and glu- ons reggeize, the dispersive representation of this ampli- tude requires a subtraction. We assume that the asymp- totic behaviour is dominated by Reggeon exchange. This leads to a sum rule that expresses the subtraction function in terms of measurable quantities. The evalua- tion of this sum rule leads to mp−n QED = 0.58 ± 0.16 MeV.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 2 Lorentz invariance, kinematic zeros; 3 Dispersion relations; 4 Reggeon dominance; 5 Sum rule for the subtraction function; 6 Wick rotation; 7 Decomposition of the mass shift; 8 Operator product expansion; 9 Leading Wilson coefficients; 10 Difference between proton and neutron; 11 Perturbation theory; 12 Moments of the structure functions; 13 Prediction for the constant C; 14 Renormalization; 15 Subleading divergence; 16 Input used for the structure functions; 17 Polarizabilities S(0)",
      "lang": "en",
